{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2608.05800, what are two distinct reasons why a hiring model's prediction may not justify excluding an applicant, and what must institutions do in such cases?", "answer": "A hiring model's prediction may be based on observed performance only among those hired and may reflect factors other than the intended construct. Institutions must justify any extension beyond the observed population and record where evidence is absent.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "Evaluation among past employees does not automatically support inference for previously excluded applicants. These are distinct problems. A record may be inaccurate, it may accurately record something other than the intended construct, or it may cover only a selected part of the population."}, {"page": 20, "quote": "Validation among hired workers does not establish performance for all applicants. Even if applicability is established, good retention prediction does not establish diligence. Nor does improved prediction establish that the proposed exclusion policy is beneficial. These are separate evidentiary gaps, not three descriptions of the same accuracy problem."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2608.05800, what are two requirements for regulatory transparency in consequential AI, and how should affected people be enabled to challenge inferences?", "answer": "Regulatory transparency must make the basis for assessing validity available for scrutiny and enable affected people and reviewers to challenge the inference and its role in the decision.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "We accordingly argue that regulatory transparency must make the basis for assessing validity available for scrutiny. This includes the meaning of the target, the justification for its measurement, the assumptions connecting observations to conclusions, and the evidence supporting use in the intended population and setting. Where competing interpretations remain compatible with the observations, disclosure of the model's computation cannot resolve that uncertainty (Section 3.2). Transparency must expose these limits and enable affected people and reviewers to challenge the inference and its role in the decision."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2608.05800, what is the statistical consequence of omitting a relevant variable from a regression model when both the omitted variable and its covariance with the included variable are positive?", "answer": "Omitting a relevant variable with positive effect and positive covariance with the included variable increases the regression coefficient on the included variable relative to the fuller model.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "If both β2 and Cov(X1, X2) are positive, this slope is larger than β1, the coefficient in the fuller model. The distinction matters even when the model predicts accurately: the fitted association does not by itself justify the interpretation attached to its coefficient."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2608.05800, what does the term 'collider' refer to in the context of causal analysis, and what risk does conditioning on a collider introduce?", "answer": "A collider is a variable that is a common effect of two others; conditioning on it can introduce bias into a causal analysis.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "Conditioning on a variable that is a common effect of two others-a collider-can itself introduce bias into a causal analysis (Pearl, 2009)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2608.05800, what is a key reason that evaluating only isolated answers from patient-facing medical chatbots is insufficient for deployment?", "answer": "Evaluating only isolated answers is insufficient because a clinically appropriate recommendation depends on obtaining relevant information, interpreting it correctly, and communicating guidance the patient can use.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "A clinically appropriate recommendation depends on obtaining relevant information, interpreting it correctly, and communicating guidance that the patient can understand and use. Validation must therefore examine complete interactions in the intended population and setting, including recognition of uncertainty, elicitation of missing information, and timely escalation to clinical care."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2608.05800, what is the main regulatory implication of the Validity Case for institutions making consequential claims about individuals?", "answer": "Institutions must justify what they claim to know about a person before that claim can justify how the person is treated.", "evidence": [{"page": 21, "quote": "An institution must justify what it claims to know about a person before that claim can justify how the person is treated."}]}
]}
